        74.67749786376953,
        324.4253845214844
      ],
      "text_sha256": "7079b45d9e853c6b1667606517c7e346a8ac503ce430d23a01057b2a1ad27941"
    },
    {
      "block_id": "page-0618-block-0018",
      "order": 18,
      "text": "10–20mg\n15–30 minutes \nIM\n",
      "bbox": [
        117.0,
        305.72039794921875,
        262.0199890136719,
        324.4253845214844
      ],
      "text_sha256": "b9b063ff50fc4a0fac644aa6b9605f52bc333a7123664324141f5659bb1d130c"
    },
    {
      "block_id": "page-0618-block-0019",
      "order": 19,
      "text": "Apparently effective. QT prolongation is of \nconcern in this patient group\n",
      "bbox": [
        274.0050048828125,
        305.72039794921875,
        411.945068359375,
        324.4253845214844
      ],
      "text_sha256": "c4c1dabf5bc8343610ed5d14a6dd020744ad905a1ff195efae6ab14def38117c"
    },
    {
      "block_id": "page-0618-block-0020",
      "order": 20,
      "text": "ECG essential\n",
      "bbox": [
        274.0050048828125,
        331.5353698730469,
        321.0975036621094,
        340.4228820800781
      ],
      "text_sha256": "fc998b385938404c4bed719d9aa6baba52601465880e2bf974319bbc0f0c0746"
    },
    {
      "block_id": "page-0618-block-0021",
      "order": 21,
      "text": "Aripiprazole IM20,21\n9.75mg\n15–30 minutes\nEvidence of effectiveness in adults but no \nclinical trial data for children and adolescents\n",
      "bbox": [
        48.00750732421875,
        346.7378845214844,
        417.0,
        365.4478759765625
      ],
      "text_sha256": "dd4f3dafcaf00228beb59be78e3afd1fa498fdf3328ce60f4d901ee2c52d0e70"
    },
    {
      "block_id": "page-0618-block-0022",
      "order": 22,
      "text": "Promethazine IM\n<12 years: 5–25mg\n(max 50mg/day)\n",
      "bbox": [
        48.00750732421875,
        371.76287841796875,
        179.0250244140625,
        390.4678649902344
      ],
      "text_sha256": "b833a7ae8864ade275774fb076532daa9bdc8d3a78e64d7fcc130480d3e1c02a"
    },
    {
      "block_id": "page-0618-block-0023",
      "order": 23,
      "text": ">12 years: 25–50mg\n(max 100mg/day)\n",
      "bbox": [
        117.00750732421875,
        397.75787353515625,
        183.19500732421875,
        416.4628601074219
      ],
      "text_sha256": "a54ce15b1c5cd7e291bfe03afd2cd28e5aff76b5fd51abcc5ac66721060e4563"
    },
    {
      "block_id": "page-0618-block-0024",
      "order": 24,
      "text": "Up to 60 minutes\nAn effective sedative, although has a slow \nonset of action. Useful if the cause of \nbehavioural disturbance is unknown and there \nis concern about the use of antipsychotic \nmedication in a child or young person\n",
      "bbox": [
        212.010009765625,
        371.75537109375,
        416.2726745605469,
        420.4528503417969
      ],
      "text_sha256": "7ec9d06dd6b4c15ad5b6b26da5702147680a88192d74f1a535852482289de30f"
    },
    {
      "block_id": "page-0618-block-0025",
      "order": 25,
      "text": "*Note that young people are particularly vulnerable to disinhibitory reactions with benzodiazepines.\n",
      "bbox": [
        48.0150146484375,
        430.74285888671875,
        366.1199951171875,
        439.4503479003906
      ],
      "text_sha256": "37c8f14237590027df372be1c9b099910cb7d30e7831df27284e8b9ad57f5cb6"
    },
    {
      "block_id": "page-0618-block-0026",
      "order": 26,
      "text": "Oral medication should always be offered (and repeated if necessary if the young \nperson is willing to take it), before resorting to parenteral treatment. Buccal mida-\nzolam14 and inhaled loxapine22 have not been widely investigated in children in RT at \nthe time of writing and have limited availability. Buccal midazolam is commonly used \nfor seizures in children. Monitoring after RT is the same as in adults (see section on RT, \nChapter 3).\n",
      "bbox": [
        47.99749755859375,
        463.7139892578125,
        422.8074035644531,
        535.4340209960938
      ],
